Accept-Encoding with -dump too: lynx left it out of every request in dump mode, to keep the <BASE> that
-source prepends right (a FIXME in HTTP.c), and so downloaded every page whole. Now only when the dump isn't the
rendered page (-source, -mime_header, -convert_to): -dump shows the same from a compressed transfer (gzip: a sixth
of the bytes for most HTML, to download and decrypt).

--- a/WWW/Library/Implementation/HTTP.c
+++ b/WWW/Library/Implementation/HTTP.c
@@ -737,8 +737,13 @@
 	 * base URL.  This is less efficient than accepting the compressed data
 	 * and uncompressing it, adding the base URL but is simpler than
 	 * augmenting the dump's presentation logic -TD
+	 *
+	 * Only when the dump isn't the rendered page (-source, -mime_header,
+	 * -convert_to set an output format): a compressed transfer doesn't
+	 * change what -dump shows.
 	 */
-	if (LYPrependBaseToSource && dump_output_immediately) {
+	if (LYPrependBaseToSource && dump_output_immediately &&
+	    HTOutputFormat != NULL) {
 	    CTRACE((tfp,
 		    "omit Accept-Encoding to work-around interaction with -source\n"));
 	} else {
